    "updated_at": "2026-11-19T18:27:25Z",
    "body": "Cause of the three refusals, from the pinned solidcore source (worker reading, not built):\n\n- solidcore makes an exact pcurve only in closed form: a curve on a plane, a circle about an axis, a ruling line, and on a spline face only a straight (u,v) line. `crates/solidcore/src/eval/pcurve.rs` says that nothing is fitted. This is an intended limit, not a defect.\n- Each refused face is on a B-spline or NURBS surface, and the failing edge is a free-form 3D curve on that face. truck fitted a pcurve there and imported the file.\n- solidcore has a seam for this: `solidcore-step` has `read_solids_with(ex, &dyn Trims)`, and `solidcore_ops::pcurve::Trims` is a kernel that makes the pcurves that have no closed form. Only tests implement `Trims`. The default read passes no `Trims`, so these faces are refused.\n- The upstream repository has no issue list and no branch for this.\n\nTwo ways to make the three fixtures read through solidcore:\n\n1. solidcore ships a production `Trims` kernel that projects or fits the pcurve and checks it by a lift to 3D.\n2. `camcraft-kernel` implements `Trims` and calls `read_solids_with`.\n\nThe two ways use a fitted pcurve. That is a design decision for the maintainer.\n\nPart that can stand alone now, without the importer switch (kernel crate only): the pin move of #2781, the `Blend` arms, the new bench verdicts, our two `twisted_flank` fixtures, and a kernel test that pins the read at V=8000. Risk that is not verified by a build: the app fixture walkers still use truck, and truck reads `twisted_flank.step` as an open shell.\n\nHeld branch: `issue/917-solidcore-step` at 776ba8b76548e3094a13534a0325332c69473039 (pushed, no PR).\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
